# The downset method

For a Heyting algebra with a nucleus $`j` and an element $`a`, the map
$`x \mapsto x \land a` is onto $`{\downarrow}a`, which is again a Heyting
algebra with a nucleus: implication $`(l \twoheadrightarrow l') \land a`,
nucleus $`\bigcirc l \land a`, top $`a` (the reduction lemmas `red_box`,
`red_imp`, `red_or`).  So $`{\downarrow}a` is generated by its bottom, and a
finite list $`L` that contains $`\bot` and $`a`, lies below $`a` and is closed up
to $`\dashv\vdash` under these operations (a `DownTable`) is all of it.  PROVED.

{stmt}`DownTable.classify`

{leanStmt DownTable.classify}

The five tables, up to $`\dashv\vdash` (`D1_table`, …, `D5_table`).  PROVED.  Their
224 interderivabilities and 17 derivabilities are closed tableaux of the
library's subframe tableau, checked by the kernel and translated into natural
deduction; the members are pairwise distinct on a four-world constraint model.

:::f0table (widths := "0.14 0.1 0.6")
* * Downset
  * Size
  * Elements
* * $`{\downarrow}t_1`
